# Predicting the downstream outcome of used diesel particulate filters from images, flow and service history: a synthetic-data study

*Rendered from run `{{ run_name }}` (profile `{{ profile }}`, code `{{ commit }}`, data manifest `{{ manifest }}`) on {{ today }}.*

> **All data is synthetic.** Every result below was produced on data from a documented simulator. It demonstrates the method and the validation approach; it is not an estimate of real-world performance.

## Abstract

Used diesel particulate filters (DPFs) are cleaned before tests decide whether they return to service, need an extended clean or are scrapped, so scrapped units consume cleaning capacity for nothing. We study a pre-inspection gate that predicts the downstream outcome before cleaning from two face images, an incoming flow-bench curve and service metadata. A simulator generates {{ s.n_units | fmt }} units with partial observability by design: some failure modes are visible only in images, some only in flow, and some (internal cracks) only indirectly. We compare classical image processing, learned defect concepts fused by gradient boosting (the *concept route*) and an end-to-end multi-modal network. On the test split, the best macro-F1 is {{ cmp.best.macro_f1.mean | fmt }} ({{ cmp.best.label }}). After calibration, split-conformal prediction sets reach {{ d.coverage["0.1"].sets_vs_recorded.coverage | pct }} coverage at a nominal 90 %. A cost-based routing policy with a wrongful-scrap cap and a minimum scrap-capture constraint captures {{ d.rates.scrap_capture_rate | pct }} of scrap before cleaning, at the price of referring {{ d.rates.share_REFER | pct }} of units to an inspector. {% if v %}An industrial validation campaign (robustness, domain shift, measurement system analysis, imaging design of experiments, latency) passes {{ v.acceptance | selectattr("status", "equalto", "PASS") | list | length }} of {{ v.acceptance | length }} illustrative acceptance criteria. {% endif %}We report every result, including those that are not favourable, and discuss what would be needed to transfer the method to real data.

## 1. Introduction

A cleaning and remanufacture line receives used DPFs from many applications and duty cycles. Today every unit receives a thermal and pneumatic clean before a flow-restriction test (pressure-drop ratio against the new-part specification) and a filtration-integrity test decide its disposition. Pre-inspection could save oven time, energy and labour by recognising scrap early, sending heavily loaded units straight to an extended clean, and asking an inspector only when the evidence is unclear. The physical background is summarised in `docs/domain_primer.md`.

Two properties make the problem harder than defect detection alone. First, the outcome depends on things the camera cannot see: ash deep in the channels, glazing, and cracks that do not reach a face. Second, the modalities interact: a crack or missing plugs open a bypass that *lowers* the measured pressure drop, so a damaged, heavily loaded filter can look normal on flow alone. The questions studied are:

1. How well can each modality, and their fusion, predict the outcome?
2. Do interpretable, measured concepts lose accuracy compared with an end-to-end network?
3. Can the prediction be turned into a safe, cost-aware routing policy with calibrated uncertainty?
4. How would such a system be validated under industrial conditions?

## 2. Related work

Segmentation uses a U-Net (Ronneberger et al., 2015) with a ResNet encoder. Unknown defects are handled by a PatchCore-style memory bank of normal patch features (Roth et al., 2022). Explanations use Grad-CAM (Selvaraju et al., 2017), Integrated Gradients (Sundararajan et al., 2017) and SHAP (Lundberg & Lee, 2017). They are tested with deletion and insertion curves (Petsiuk et al., 2018) and a model-randomisation sanity check (Adebayo et al., 2018). Probabilities are calibrated by temperature scaling (Guo et al., 2017). Uncertainty is expressed with split-conformal prediction sets, using adaptive prediction sets (Romano et al., 2020) and following the presentation of Angelopoulos & Bates. Measurement-system analysis follows the AIAG MSA Reference Manual (4th ed.), risk analysis the AIAG & VDA FMEA Handbook (2019), and AI risk management ISO/IEC 23894:2023. Documentation follows the model-card idea (Mitchell et al., 2019) and datasheets for datasets (Gebru et al., 2021).

## 3. Data: a virtual inspection cell

The simulator (`src/preinspect/synth/`, generator v{{ generator_version }}, frozen before any model was trained) draws each unit's service history from one of six applications. That history drives soot and ash load, thermal stress (and with it cracks, plug loss and melting), and oil or coolant contamination. Face images are rendered from physical layers (reflectance, relief, transmission) under three lighting modes, with seating, exposure, white-balance, noise, blur and clamp variation. Eight defect classes have pixel masks, and dust, reflections, marker ink and lighting gradients act as nuisances. The incoming flow curve follows ΔP = aQ + bQ² with loading raising both terms and leak paths lowering ΔP. A documented outcome model maps the latent condition to post-clean ΔP ratio, integrity and disposition, with test noise.

Data-quality faults are injected on purpose: missing and out-of-range values, duplicate bookings, Fahrenheit temperatures and mistyped dispositions. A validation gate quarantined {{ s.validation.n_quarantined_records | fmt }} of {{ s.validation.n_records | fmt }} records and blanked {{ s.validation.n_fields_blanked | fmt }} ambiguous temperature fields. Recorded dispositions differ from the true outcome on {{ s.label_vs_truth_disagreement | pct }} of accepted records because of test noise near the specification limit. Splits are chronological (train, calibration, test). A *novel* set contains thermal melt, a defect class absent from training, and a *shift* set has an unseen cell density and a different camera. See `docs/data_card.md`.

## 4. Methods

**Classical concepts.** The substrate is located from texture and its radius refined on the mat gap. The cell lattice comes from the spectrum (with the datasheet pitch resolving the checkerboard/wall-grid ambiguity) and a template phase search. Per-cell sampling gives missing-plug counts and blocked-cell fractions. A notch filter removes the periodic lattice, after which a Sato ridge filter and skeleton length measure cracks. The polar perimeter profile measures chips, and LAB statistics measure stains and dark (sooty) areas.

**Perception and cross-fitting.** A U-Net with a ResNet-18 encoder predicts {{ cfg.perception.classes | length }} defect classes and image-level presence. Training units are split into {{ cfg.perception.folds }} folds, each with its own U-Net and anomaly bank. A training unit's concepts come from the models that did not see it, so the outcome model learns from concepts as noisy as those it meets in service. Concepts (areas, crack length in mm, clock position, missing-plug count) are measured from the predicted masks.

**Anomaly guard.** Mid-level ResNet-18 features of defect-free training faces form a coreset memory bank, and nearest-neighbour distances give an anomaly map. The guard acts on the *unexplained* anomaly, meaning the part of the map outside regions the perception model already names, so known defects are not referred merely for being unusual.

**Concept route.** Gradient boosting (scikit-learn histogram boosting) fuses learned concepts, anomaly scores, flow features (fitted a, b, ΔP at rated flow over the new-part value) and metadata. It predicts the outcome and the post-clean ΔP ratio. A cross-modal feature compares flow restriction with what the weight gain predicts; a value below expectation indicates bypass.

**End-to-end network.** A shared ResNet-18 encodes both faces, a 1D convolution encodes the normalised flow curve, and an MLP encodes metadata with missing-value indicators. A softmax gate weights the three embeddings per unit before heads for outcome, ΔP ratio and integrity. The loss is a class-weighted multi-task loss.

**Calibration, conformal prediction and routing.** The calibration split is divided into two halves. On the first, networks are temperature-scaled; for boosting models a method (sigmoid, isotonic or temperature) is chosen by cross-validated log-loss. The same half sets the anomaly threshold and the routing thresholds. On the second half, conformal quantiles are fitted. The policy routes a unit to REFER for invalid inputs or unexplained anomaly, to QUARANTINE when P(SCRAP) exceeds a threshold, to REFER when SCRAP is in the conformal set, to EXTENDED_CLEAN when P(REWORK) exceeds a threshold, and otherwise to PROCEED. Thresholds and the routing confidence level minimise expected cost (`configs/costs.yaml`, every value an assumption) subject to a wrongful-scrap cap of {{ costs.policy.wrongful_scrap_cap.value | pct }} and a minimum scrap capture of {{ costs.policy.min_scrap_capture.value | pct }}. A template rationale explains every decision from measured values.

