## 7. Discussion

**Accuracy against interpretability.** The concept route expresses every prediction in physical quantities an inspector can check: crack length and clock position, missing-plug count, outlet-face soot, flow restriction against weight gain. That makes the rationale possible, makes commissioning a new line a perception-only task, and lets the explanation be tested against what the simulator knows. {% if cmp.concept_minus_e2e is not none and cmp.concept_minus_e2e >= 0 %}Here it costs no accuracy against the end-to-end network.{% else %}Here the end-to-end network is ahead in macro-F1, and the difference has to be weighed against those advantages.{% endif %}

**What limits scrap detection.** By design of the simulator, part of the scrap population fails integrity because of internal cracks that never reach a face. Their only signatures are outlet-face soot and a pressure drop lower than the weight gain predicts, both noisy. The safety constraint therefore forces many referrals. Better perception of thin defects (cracks, missing plugs) is the most valuable improvement, followed by a cheap direct leak measurement at the station.

**Operating point.** Macro-F1 differences between the strongest models are small, but their operating points are not. Under the same cost model and constraints, the deployed model refers {{ d.rates.share_REFER | pct }} of units{% if cmp.deployed_fewest_referrals %}, fewer than any other approach{% else %}; at least one other approach needs fewer referrals, which argues for revisiting the deployed choice{% endif %}. Choosing a model by its routing cost at the required safety level, not by a single accuracy figure, is the main practical lesson.

## 8. Threats to validity

- **Synthetic data.** The relationships between latent condition and observations are the simulator's assumptions. A model can only rediscover them, so absolute numbers mean little; relative comparisons depend on the simulator's balance of visible and invisible failure modes.
- **Simplified optics.** Stylised faces have far fewer cells than real ones; backlight is modelled per face; there is no specular texture of real cement or soot.
- **Small calibration and test sets** in the demo profile give wide confidence intervals on recall and coverage.
- **Re-use of the calibration half** for both probability calibration and threshold selection may make the chosen thresholds slightly optimistic.
- **Simulated inspectors and line.** Inspector biases and line capacities are assumptions.

## 9. Future research and development

1. Collect real data under the protocol in `docs/real_data_plan.md`: a labelled image set, flow curves and downstream test records for a few hundred units, with an inter-rater study.
2. Close the sim-to-real gap by pre-training perception on simulated faces rendered with real cell geometry, then fine-tuning on real labelled faces.
3. Add a direct leak measurement (light or smoke transmission) at the station, the strongest missing signal for internal cracks.
4. Tiled, high-resolution inference for real cell counts, and an edge deployment benchmark.
5. Active learning: route the most informative REFER cases to labelling.
6. A shadow-mode pilot that measures the gate's decisions against downstream tests without acting on them.

